WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.062 --> 00:00:07.896
They have been called "a girl's best friend" and "a symbol of undying love." They have been a sign of status and wealth for centuries.

2
00:00:07.913 --> 00:00:16.065
What are they? Diamonds, of course. But diamonds are not just "a pretty face"; they are a valuable commodity.

3
00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:27.384
Diamonds are a form of carbon, but unlike other forms of carbon such as graphite (the substance used as pencil lead), diamonds are created in very unusual conditions.

4
00:00:27.385 --> 00:00:41.535
Scientists estimate that diamonds are formed over millennia, 150 to 670 kilometers beneath the earth's surface, where the extremely high pressure and temperatures needed to crystallize carbon into diamond exist.

5
00:00:41.536 --> 00:00:49.649
Thus, they are extremely old—at minimum, millions of years old, with some as much as three billion years old.

6
00:00:49.650 --> 00:01:02.346
Meteorites can also create diamonds when they slam into the earth's surface, producing in the impact the necessary temperatures of 1,000 to 1,200 degrees Celsius and extremely high pressure.

7
00:01:02.347 --> 00:01:11.681
Another type of diamond, black or gray opaque stones called carbonados, was created in space and carried to earth by meteorites.

8
00:01:11.682 --> 00:01:15.893
Diamonds are brought from the depths of the earth to the surface in many ways.

9
00:01:15.894 --> 00:01:20.624
Sometimes, magma carries them upward from deep within the earth's crust.

10
00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:28.732
In certain areas, magma flowing to the surface has formed tunnels, or "pipes," that include a high concentration of diamonds.

11
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:35.393
These are commonly called "kimberlite pipes," after the first one discovered in Kimberley, South Africa.

12
00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:43.044
Most diamonds are mined from kimberlite pipes in South Africa, Russia, Australia, Botswana, and Zaire.

13
00:01:43.045 --> 00:01:49.703
Other methods have been created to find diamonds washed into waterways through erosion or landslides.

14
00:01:49.704 --> 00:01:54.596
Diamond mining, unfortunately, brings with it many environmental concerns.

15
00:01:54.597 --> 00:02:02.366
For instance, the chemical leftovers of the mining process are often dumped into rivers and streams, polluting the water.

16
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:09.255
Another concern within the diamond industry involves conflict diamonds, also known as blood diamonds.

17
00:02:09.256 --> 00:02:18.394
These are diamonds mined and sold to finance wars and violent revolutions. Blood diamonds are also linked to child slavery and child soldiers.

18
00:02:18.395 --> 00:02:30.131
To make it more difficult to finance war through diamond sales, the diamond industry has organized a certification system to establish that a diamond has not come from a conflict region.

19
00:02:30.132 --> 00:02:42.892
The system has been largely effective because De Beers, a South African company, controls more than half the worldwide supply and sales of diamonds and authorizes only a limited number of buyers to acquire diamonds.

20
00:02:42.893 --> 00:02:47.947
Diamonds continue to be popular because of their extraordinary physical properties.

21
00:02:47.948 --> 00:02:57.812
With a rating of ten on the Mohs hardness scale, which measures the relative hardness of minerals, diamonds are the hardest known naturally occurring substance.

22
00:02:57.813 --> 00:03:01.979
Thus, a diamond can only be scratched by another diamond.

23
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:08.140
Diamonds also rate highly in brilliance, which refers to how much light a gemstone reflects.

24
00:03:08.141 --> 00:03:17.751
Another property for which diamonds are noted is fire, which refers to the way light disperses into the full spectrum of colors when it passes through a gemstone.

25
00:03:17.752 --> 00:03:22.863
White light passing through a diamond is separated into all the colors of the rainbow.

26
00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:28.347
Because of these flashes of color, diamonds are said to have a very high degree of fire.

27
00:03:28.348 --> 00:03:33.572
The greater the brilliance and fire of a particular diamond, the greater its value.

28
00:03:33.573 --> 00:03:38.564
Most people are familiar with clear diamonds, as these are most popular.

29
00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:48.494
Diamonds have many levels of transparency, however, and come in a wide range of colors—including green, blue, red, orange, yellow, and brown.

30
00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:54.335
The rarer colored diamonds are known as "fancies" and are more expensive than white diamonds.

31
00:03:54.336 --> 00:04:04.429
Only the highest-quality diamonds are selected for jewelry. Diamonds are judged according to the "four Cs": color, clarity, carat, and cut.

32
00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:12.107
Color refers to the hue of the diamond. Colorless diamonds are sought after due to their image of purity and sophistication.

33
00:04:12.108 --> 00:04:16.940
Fancies that exhibit a pure color are highly esteemed and very valuable.

34
00:04:16.941 --> 00:04:22.646
On the other hand, diamonds with tinges of yellow are usually not used as gems at all.

35
00:04:22.647 --> 00:04:27.146
Clarity indicates whether there are internal blemishes or flaws in the stone.

36
00:04:27.147 --> 00:04:34.849
Veins or other visible imperfections will drive down the value of a diamond. Carat refers to a diamond's mass.

37
00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:42.167
One carat equals two hundred milligrams. Obviously, the bigger the diamond, the higher its price tag.

38
00:04:42.168 --> 00:04:47.271
Cut has to do with how the diamond is faceted to show off its fire and brilliance.

39
00:04:47.272 --> 00:04:53.647
The cut of a diamond can either enhance its natural beauty or, if poorly done, smother it.

40
00:04:53.648 --> 00:04:59.981
Taking a rough, or uncut, diamond and transforming it into a gemstone is a painstaking process.

41
00:04:59.982 --> 00:05:03.696
A rough diamond can be hard to distinguish from other stones.

42
00:05:03.697 --> 00:05:10.969
Only after the process of cleaving, cutting, and polishing is a diamond transformed into a dazzling gem.

43
00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:19.796
Before a diamond cutter can begin, he or she will carefully examine the stone to see how it can best be cut to maximize its beauty and value.

44
00:05:19.797 --> 00:05:29.121
It is easier and less wasteful to split the diamond along planes that are naturally weaker, so diamond cutters first divide the stone along those lines.

45
00:05:29.122 --> 00:05:38.005
Then, following a carefully devised plan, the cutter will make facets. Often, about half the diamond is cut away during this process.

46
00:05:38.006 --> 00:05:46.952
The most popular design, the "brilliant" cut, usually has 58 facets and allows the stone to reflect the maximum amount of light.

47
00:05:46.953 --> 00:05:54.165
While the diamonds most people are familiar with are in the form of gems, not all diamonds are processed into jewelry.

48
00:05:54.166 --> 00:05:58.872
In fact, 80 percent of diamonds are used in industrial processes.

49
00:05:58.873 --> 00:06:09.762
Diamonds with flaws in color or shape, or without the brilliance and fire necessary to capture the eye and sell as gemstones, are employed in a variety of manufacturing sectors.

50
00:06:09.763 --> 00:06:17.968
Some are used as a single crystal, while others are ground into a fine powder and applied to saw blades and the tips of drills.

51
00:06:17.969 --> 00:06:24.356
Because diamonds are so hard, they can cut, scratch, scrape, and drill through all other surfaces.

52
00:06:24.357 --> 00:06:31.960
Diamonds are used not only in mining, but also in the oil and gas industries, electronics, and even dentistry.

53
00:06:31.961 --> 00:06:37.404
In an effort to keep up with the demand for diamonds, synthetic diamonds have been developed.

54
00:06:37.405 --> 00:06:42.344
The majority of these synthetic diamonds are suitable only for industrial purposes.

55
00:06:42.345 --> 00:06:49.501
Only 10 percent of industrial-grade diamonds are natural diamonds; the other 90 percent are synthetic.

56
00:06:49.502 --> 00:06:53.065
Some artificial diamonds are also used for jewelry.

57
00:06:53.066 --> 00:06:58.313
Diamonds are known all over the world as precious stones that symbolize love and romance.

58
00:06:58.314 --> 00:07:05.320
But diamonds also have strength beneath their beauty. The diamond is much more than just another pretty face.

